This repository contains files writen in .tex (LaTeX language)

All of the files are sketches regarding some prolems i find on books and
other sources that i want to solve in a greater depth of mathematical
thinking.

For reference, read [the LaTeX Wikibook](https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/LaTeX) and [the LaTeX project website](https://www.latex-project.org/).

## Guides to solving problems using logic

* [How to Solve It](https://www.amazon.com/How-Solve-Mathematical-Princeton-Science/dp/069116407X) by George Pólya is the foundational classic on mathematical problem-solving, teaching general heuristics and strategies applicable across all areas of mathematics.
* [The art of solving problems](https://www.amazon.com/Art-Solving-Problems-Classic-Mathematical/dp/0471018192) by Russell L. Ackoff is a great book that teaches you how to solve problems using logic and critical thinking.
* [Problems in General Physics](https://www.amazon.com/Problems-General-Physics-I-Irodov/dp/8183552153) by I. E. Irodov is the legendary collection of physics problems requiring deep mathematical thinking, widely used by serious physics students worldwide.
* [Street-Fighting Mathematics](https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262514293/street-fighting-mathematics/) by Sanjoy Mahajan teaches the art of educated guessing and opportunistic problem-solving — reasoning your way to good answers without brute force.

## Mathematical Logic

* [Introduction to Mathematical Logic](https://www.amazon.com/Introduction-Mathematical-Logic-Elliott-Mendelson/dp/1482237725) by Elliott Mendelson is the standard rigorous introduction to propositional calculus, first-order logic, formal number theory, and set theory.
* [Mathematical Logic](https://www.amazon.com/Mathematical-Logic-Dover-Books-Mathematics/dp/0486264041) by Stephen Cole Kleene is a comprehensive and precise treatment of the foundations of logic, covering formal systems, recursive functions, and the incompleteness theorems.
* [Gödel, Escher, Bach: An Eternal Golden Braid](https://www.amazon.com/Godel-Escher-Bach-Eternal-Golden/dp/0465026567) by Douglas Hofstadter explores self-reference, formal systems, and the meaning of Gödel's incompleteness theorems through a playful and deeply profound lens.
* [Set Theory and the Continuum Hypothesis](https://www.amazon.com/Set-Theory-Continuum-Hypothesis-Mathematics/dp/0486469212) by Paul J. Cohen presents the independence proofs that shook the foundations of mathematics, written by the mathematician who invented forcing.
* [Logic and Structure](https://www.amazon.com/Logic-Structure-Universitext-Dirk-Dalen/dp/1447145577) by Dirk van Dalen is a clean, modern introduction to propositional and predicate logic with natural deduction, ideal for mathematicians.

## Physics Problems and Mathematical Methods

* [Thinking Physics](https://www.amazon.com/Thinking-Physics-Understandable-Practical-Reality/dp/0935218084) by Lewis Carroll Epstein builds physical intuition through conceptual problems, training you to see what the mathematics must say before you write it down.
* [Mathematical Methods for Physics and Engineering](https://www.amazon.com/Mathematical-Methods-Physics-Engineering-Comprehensive/dp/0521679710) by Riley, Hobson & Bence is the comprehensive reference for the mathematical toolkit every physics researcher needs — from vector calculus to group theory.
* [The Feynman Lectures on Physics](https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/) by Feynman, Leighton & Sands remains the gold standard for understanding physics deeply rather than mechanically; available free online.
* [A Guide to Physics Problems](https://www.amazon.com/Guide-Physics-Problems-Mechanics-Relativity/dp/0306484013) by Cahn & Nadgorny (2 vols.) collects graduate-level problems from top university qualifying exams, with full solutions and commentary.

## Becoming a Full Researcher

* [The Craft of Research](https://www.amazon.com/Research-Chicago-Writing-Editing-Publishing/dp/022623973X) by Booth, Colomb & Williams is the definitive guide to formulating research questions, making arguments, and presenting evidence — essential reading regardless of field.
* [How to Write Mathematics](https://www.amazon.com/How-Write-Mathematics-Norman-Steenrod/dp/0821800558) by Steenrod, Halmos, Schiffer & Dieudonné teaches the craft of writing clear, rigorous, and readable mathematics from four grandmasters of exposition.
* [Writing Your Dissertation in Fifteen Minutes a Day](https://www.amazon.com/Writing-Your-Dissertation-Fifteen-Minutes/dp/0805048lead) by Joan Bolker addresses the psychological and practical sides of sustaining long-form academic work.
* [A Mathematician's Survival Guide](https://www.amazon.com/Mathematicians-Survival-Guide-Graduate-School/dp/082183455X) by Steven Krantz covers graduate school, writing papers, giving talks, finding jobs, and navigating the culture of academic mathematics.
* [Mathematics and Plausible Reasoning](https://www.amazon.com/Mathematics-Plausible-Reasoning-Induction-Analogy/dp/0691025096) (2 vols.) by George Pólya teaches how to develop mathematical conjectures and build intuition — the indispensable complement to formal proof-writing.